1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:08,090
So when you are the director, um, overseeing these massive digital systems for your ministry,

2
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:10,920
I think you eventually realize something pretty quickly.

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,260
You aren't really fighting bad technology.

4
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:14,760
You're fighting fragmentation.

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:16,000
Oh, absolutely. Yeah.

6
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:24,840
Like one department spends millions building a system to register citizens, and, and then another ministry builds their own system

7
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,800
to, you know, register those exact same citizens.

8
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:32,619
Right. And the result is that the people you're trying to serve are stuck filling out the exact same form, what?

9
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,260
Five times at five different counters?

10
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:44,220
Yeah, exactly. It happens constantly. And I, I mean, from the outside, the obvious substantive question is: Shouldn't the people running these individual programs realize the duplication and just, you

11
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:45,540
know, fix it internally?

12
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,100
Well, see, that's the thing. They physically cannot.

13
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:50,400
Wait, really? Why not?

14
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:53,320
Because each program is just doing exactly what it was funded to do.

15
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:56,810
I mean, they aren't funded to build some unified national infrastructure, right?

16
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:01,160
They're funded to build a school list or, uh, a tax portal.

17
00:01:01,449 --> 00:01:05,700
Ah, so the true issue isn't a lack of technical effort inside the program.

18
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:10,619
No, not at all. It's the complete absence of a shared overarching plan.

19
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:13,520
Without that, every new initiative operates in a vacuum.

20
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:16,660
You just end up rebuilding what the ministry next door already built.

21
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,940
Okay, so let's unpack how a government actually falls into this trap.

22
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:26,760
What does this fragmentation actually look like on the ground for, uh, for the people running these programs?

23
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:30,940
So there are four critical signs that your government lacks a shared plan.

24
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,020
Sign one is multiple registers for the same people.

25
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,180
Okay, multiple registers. Like what?

26
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:36,620
Like

27
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:44,500
your ministry might have a social register, while the education department has a school list, and a third agency has the national ID register.

28
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:48,740
And because nobody agreed on a shared data standard beforehand,

29
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:51,420
none of these registers agree with each other.

30
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:53,370
Oh, wow, so they literally don't match.

31
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:58,980
Right. Which brings us to sign two: building system connections from zero every single time.

32
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:02,140
Because they don't match, you can't just flip a switch to share the information.

33
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:07,860
Exactly. Every time two systems need to communicate, you have to build a custom bridge from scratch.

34
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:12,049
For example, connecting the tax office to the business register last year.

35
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:15,600
Right. And then doing the exact same work from scratch

36
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:19,680
for the health ministry this year. It's incredibly inefficient.

37
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:22,020
Which I guess leads to the vendor issue.

38
00:02:22,090 --> 00:02:26,269
Right. Because if you're relying on all this bespoke, undocumented code...

39
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,070
Yes. Sign three. Yeah. Vendor lock-in on ten-year-old systems.

40
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:34,940
Yeah. Where the vendor dictates the price because literally nobody else knows how the thing works.

41
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:38,340
Exactly. It's like a foreign language only the original developer speaks.

42
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:40,870
Yeah. And then finally, sign four:

43
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:43,480
ministries that just don't connect at all.

44
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:48,329
Making the, uh, the once only principle completely impossible in practice.

45
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:56,600
Right. So a citizen gives her ID to the health ministry, but then she still has to provide it all over again on paper to enroll her child in school.

46
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:03,120
It's like hiring specialized contractors to build a house, but, um, none of them are allowed to look at the same master blueprint.

47
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:06,460
That's exactly it. Yeah. And the costs of this pattern run in four directions.

48
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,820
Okay, let's go through the costs.

49
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:10,940
First is money. You're just repeatedly rebuilding systems.

50
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,870
Second is time. You're constantly waiting for unplanned cross-system work.

51
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:17,500
Right. Making those custom bridges you mentioned.

52
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:22,320
Yeah. Third is the citizens, obviously, who are burdened with repetitive forms.

53
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:26,760
But the fourth cost is the one that really catches leadership off guard.

54
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:29,340
And what's that? Your minister. Wait, your minister?

55
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:33,240
I would assume the minister is somewhat insulated from, like, database glitches.

56
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:36,040
They're insulated from small glitches, sure.

57
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:41,840
But your minister becomes entirely unable to deliver flagship cross-ministry programs.

58
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,920
Oh, because the systems won't talk to each other.

59
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:48,520
Exactly. If the technology is fragmented, the flagship policy fails.

60
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:50,019
The minister can't deliver.

61
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:57,720
So, okay, if the root cause is that nobody has written down what the systems are or how they fit together, what does the actual fix look like?

62
00:03:57,980 --> 00:04:01,240
The solution is a national enterprise architecture or EA.

63
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:02,920
An enterprise architecture?

64
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:06,120
Yeah. It serves as that missing shared plan.

65
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:08,560
We're talking about frameworks like PERA.

66
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:13,560
That's P-A-E-R-A, the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture,

67
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:17,260
or, um, the European Interoperability Framework.

68
00:04:17,399 --> 00:04:19,899
Okay, so it's not like you're buying a mega software platform.

69
00:04:19,959 --> 00:04:22,320
No, no. They aren't software. They're structural blueprints.

70
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:26,800
Got it. So going back to that house analogy, it's like city zoning laws.

71
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:35,520
A private developer can build whatever house they want, but the zoning law dictates that all the plumbing has to connect to the main sewer line using a standard pipe.

72
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:40,040
Yes, exactly. You aren't micromanaging the interior design of the system.

73
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,240
You're just standardizing the connections.

74
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:47,300
An EA establishes the data models every new government system must follow.

75
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:48,920
Which breaks the vendor lock-in.

76
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,760
Right, because it's built on open shared standards.

77
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:54,020
Systems can inherently communicate from day one.

78
00:04:54,240 --> 00:05:03,740
So by adopting a national enterprise architecture, you stop bleeding money on duplicate builds, you get your leverage back from vendors, and you finally make the once only

79
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:06,740
principle a reality for citizens.

80
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,760
It really is the only way to build digital infrastructure that actually scales.

